NTAD National Highway System — US Government work, free redistribution WITH a
condition
  src/reference/corridor.json   (the `centerline` array: 11,924 vertices + measures)
  src/sql/002-corridor-real.sql (the same geometry as PostGIS WKT)
  both generated by src/scripts/fetch-arnold.py

Upstream: https://services.arcgis.com/xOi1kZaI0eWDREZv/arcgis/rest/services
          /NTAD_National_Highway_System/FeatureServer/0
Publisher: USDOT Bureau of Transportation Statistics; source Federal Highway
           Administration. National Transportation Atlas Database.

THE METADATA ENTRY BELOW IS THE CONDITION, and reproducing it here is how this
distribution satisfies it. Quoted verbatim from the layer's own copyrightText,
where it is carried in-band with the data:

  "The NHS Version 2025.08.08 database, or any portion thereof, can be freely
  distributed as long as this metadata entry is included with each distribution.
  The original metadata entry cannot be modified or deleted from any data
  transfer. This NTAD dataset is a work of the United States government as
  defined in 17 U.S.C. section 101 and as such are not protected by any U.S.
  copyrights. This work is available for unrestricted public use."

scripts/fetch-arnold.py asserts that text is still present before it fetches
anything (see verify_license and NTAD_LICENSE_FRAGMENT), so an upstream
relicensing fails the build rather than silently invalidating this file.

WHY A FEDERAL SOURCE RATHER THAN THE STATES' OWN. The four state DOT LRS layers
(ADOT, NMDOT, TxDOT, ODOT) carry the same route and a better per-vertex measure,
and this repository used to read them. None of them licenses redistribution:
TxDOT asserts copyright and requires WRITTEN CONSENT before the data is passed to
a third party, ODOT publishes "Authorized reference use only", NMDOT marks it
"custom", and ADOT publishes no licence metadata at all. MIT-0 purports to grant
every reader the right to copy, sell and sublicense, so shipping geometry derived
from those layers would have handed readers rights the DOTs withheld. The states
author this data and submit it to FHWA; the federal compilation is a government
work, which is the same reasoning applied to the National Bridge Inventory below.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Oklahoma milepost offsets — 19 scalars from the ODOT sign inventory
  src/reference/corridor.json, src/sql/002-corridor-real.sql (the Oklahoma leg's
  measures only; the geometry itself is NTAD's, above)

Oklahoma is the one state whose mileposts are not in any federal source, because
ODOT REFERENCES BY CONTROL SECTION AND PUBLISHES NO STATEWIDE MILEPOST MEASURE AT
ALL - NTAD, NHPN and the NBI all carry the same control-section keys. Its posted
mileposts exist only as a physical sign inventory:

  https://services6.arcgis.com/RBtoEUQ2lmN0K3GY/arcgis/rest/services
      /Signs__2021_Mile_Marker_View/FeatureServer/0

ODOT does not license redistribution, so what is taken from it is 19 SCALAR
OFFSETS and no geometry - each the median of (posted milepost - control-section
measure) over that section's markers. The position of the resulting line is
NTAD's; ODOT contributes only where each control section starts.

THE REASONING, so a reader can disagree with it: a posted milepost number on a
physical sign beside a public road is a fact observable by anyone who drives past
it, and copyright protects compilations of expression rather than individual
facts. 19 numbers is not ODOT's inventory. This is a considered line, not a
settled one, and anyone who wants Oklahoma sourced differently has what they need
to redraw it - see _calibrate_oklahoma() in scripts/fetch-arnold.py.

The same reasoning covers the seven probe coordinates in
src/sql/checks/landmarks.sql, resolved from state milepost-marker layers: seven
physical sign locations, used to check the corridor rather than to describe it.

